CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight
SCALE_REALISM: Holds for the packet's synthetic sustained-load cases, including the 3-node router topology at N=200000, which is enough to support the per-hop-not-end-to-end mechanism. Gaps remain: localhost TCP, 8B payloads, slow synchronous subscriber callback, and no demonstrated ROS 2 production workload or WAN/multi-router deployment. Offload-to-channel -> 0% loss narrows the claim to callback/transport congestion rather than general message loss.
RATIONALE: YELLOW, not GREEN, because a shipping system already implements the mechanism; the contribution is the empirical identification of a harmful composition in zenoh 1.9.0, not a new systems design. The packet's source claims are consistent with public zenoh source: DEFAULT_PUSH=Drop and DEFAULT_REQUEST=Block in core/mod.rs, CongestionControl::Block is documented as "the node will wait" at https://docs.rs/zenoh/1.9.0/zenoh/qos/enum.CongestionControl.html, and the transport pipeline has a droppable congestion path returning Ok(false). The 3-node result is above generic "best-effort drops" because it specifically tests the documented Block knob across a router hop and observes put_ok=200000 with 51.0-63.8% loss. However, artifact reproducibility is only partly established in the packet: pinned versions and measurements are present, but no retrievable artifact/script URL is provided. Prior-art search across zenoh docs/source/issues, ROS rmw_zenoh, vLLM, SGLang, LMCache, FlashInfer, TensorRT-LLM, Triton, and HF TGI did not find an independent prior report of this exact split-default + publisher-Block-not-end-to-end + silent put-Ok composition; zenoh issue #1494 is a narrower fragment-drop issue: https://github.com/eclipse-zenoh/zenoh/issues/1494. Prompt version: v001.
